{
  "dom_id": "step-q275350",
  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "Let $r=⟨-4,-1,2⟩$, $q=⟨-2,3,1⟩$ and $p=⟨5,-2,-1⟩.$ Find $r\\cdot (q\\times p)$ using a determinant.\n\n$r\\cdot (q\\times p)=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "Remember that the scalar triple product $r\\cdot (q\\times p)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $r,$ $q,$ and $p.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot (q\\times p) & =|\\begin{aligned}-4 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-4)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}|-(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}|+2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-4)(3\\cdot (-1)-(-2)\\cdot 1)+((-2)\\cdot (-1)-5\\cdot 1)+2((-2)\\cdot (-2)-5\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =(-4)(-3+2)+(2-5)+2(4-15) \\\\  & =4-3-22 \\\\  & =-21.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [
